  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Healthcare Access in Yemen (SHAY)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Yemen):  With FY2024 funds, Strengthening Healthcare Access in Yemen will deepen healthcare coverage and service quality. It will strengthen referral networks for higher levels of Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, and Child Health (RMNCH) care, increase access to and quality of family planning services, strengthen district and local level health governance and healthcare quality and expand community-based approaches to reach vulnerable populations, with the goal of reducing mortality among women of reproductive age, their newborns and children under five as well as increasing community and health system resilience. .The activity will focus on interventions that optimize RMNCH outcomes, taking an integrated approach by focusing on the Minimum Service Package (MSP). This will be coupled with appropriate support services that address life-threatening challenges for communities (e.g. malnutrition, cholera, diarrhea), including immunization and family planning, and encourage strong community engagement to improve health seeking behaviors and strengthen health systems to create local ownership to sustain system enhancements post-project.  .The activity will promote community outreach and engagement with quality, evidence-based messages via direct community engagement with local leaders and health care workers as well as media to introduce and reinforce key messages via community health counseling and services. It will work in the community with the help of community midwives (CMWs) and in health care facilities to increase the availability of the MSP in targeted districts through a robust referral system. It will also build the skills of those midwives and strengthen their links to health care facilities. .The activity will also support sustainable health system improvements through building health management information systems that support better decision-making at the district and national levels....</narrative>
  </description>
